{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Check for a common factor first — always take out the HCF",
   "For x² + bx + c: find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b",
   "For difference of two squares: use a² − b² = (a+b)(a−b)",
   "Check your answer by expanding back"
  ],
  "title": "How to Factorise Algebraic Expressions",
  "content": "<p><strong>Factorising</strong> is the reverse of expanding brackets. You write an expression as a product of factors.</p><p><strong>Single bracket (HCF):</strong> Find the highest common factor of all terms and take it outside the bracket. E.g. \\(6x + 15 = 3(2x + 5)\\).</p><p><strong>Quadratic trinomials</strong> \\(x^2 + bx + c\\): Find two numbers that multiply to \\(c\\) and add to \\(b\\). E.g. \\(x^2 + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4)\\) because \\(3 \\times 4 = 12\\) and \\(3 + 4 = 7\\).</p><p><strong>Difference of two squares:</strong> \\(a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b)\\). E.g. \\(x^2 - 25 = (x + 5)(x - 5)\\).</p><p><strong>Grouping:</strong> For four terms, group into pairs, factorise each pair, then take out the common bracket.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Factorise</strong> \\(x^2 - 3x - 10\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find two numbers that multiply to \\(-10\\) and add to \\(-3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(-5 \\times 2 = -10\\) and \\(-5 + 2 = -3\\) ✔</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\((x - 5)(x + 2)\\)</p>"
 },
